!!! The [[3 Rs|The 3Rs]] (sometimes I call it [[Post3Rs]], since the output is often poster-y stuff)

Its three different meeting days of in class design charettes based on the "3 Rs" -- [[Reduce]], [[Reuse]], [[Recycle]].

The first day students are asked to take a project and ponder how a design can be "[[Reduce]]d" -- strategies for lessening material and energy -- can you [[Reduce]] the colors or the trim size or the file size or the content? and then that becomes a good way to have a conversation about the de materialization of objects and what other forms of communicating could this design take? Its also an opportunity to talk about minimalism not as an aesthetic, but as a formal tool for minimizing material in a design for embedded energy's sake; get into the dieter rams less but better... and start asking the question "do you need to make these things at all!!??"

So we [[Reduce]] one day, and then the next class they bring in a bunch of old posters, books, preferably their own designs, but it can be any found materials... We then make "[[Reuse]]d" posters conveying some message they want to reach people with... I'm defining [[Reuse]] as Strategies for making solutions last longer & finding other uses when finished. This is more about thinking about literally physically reusing design to make new design; but it also always turns into a conversation about more general ways of reusing -- frameworks; tools; color palettes; etc.

And then the last part is [[Recycle]]; Its important to make the distinction between [[Reuse]] and recycling -- [[Reuse]] means you [[Reuse]] materials in their present state. [[Recycle]] means you return materials to a virgin state... [[Reuse]] should be less work/energy than recycling. So by recycling for the last day I mean Strategies for reclaiming as much residual value as possible & preventing virgin materials from needlessly being used -- returning used materials back into raw materials. This is an interesting design prompt -- and I go about this by asking the students to try and break down the last few projects they've made for other classes -- what content, images, typefaces, etc. did they create/make? what stuff did they make/create that they didn't end up using? and then we create a giant digital pile of all that stuff and they have to make new things from the old -- its still a little [[Reuse]]-y but I think by trying to get them to conceptually break the things down to their "raw materials" we have some interesting conversations about graphic design, our materials, our semantic choices, the symbolism of things; and what really is the "raw material" of communicating anyway... is it just signs on substrates?

Here's another documentary on remixing... http://www.nfb.ca/film/rip_a_remix_manifesto/

Its actually more interesting if you're into copyright law … and includes a lot more detail and a lot more "experts" … but isn't as easily watchable/digestible as Everything is a Remix... but it includes a good manifesto: the remixer manifesto The manifesto itself: [[The Remix Manifesto]].

I can talk a little bit about my interests in vernacular and open source, free culture through the ages — all stuff that's tangential to the idea of "ReMixing"

